{"body":"Rust crates that read `OUT_DIR` can now reuse cached compilations across checkouts when their build-script output and other compilation inputs match. Previously, the checkout-specific `OUT_DIR` value caused a cache miss even when the generated files were identical.\n\nFor example, a crate that includes generated Rust code can reuse its artifact in a second worktree:\n\n```rust\ninclude!(concat!(env!(\"OUT_DIR\"), \"/generated.rs\"));\n```\n\nmbx copies eligible build-script output to `out-dirs/v1/<digest>` under its cache and supplies that path to rustc as `OUT_DIR`. Matching output trees give rustc the same path, including when code stores or derives a value from it. The literal path remains part of the cache key; different output trees use different keys.\n\n### Configuration and compatibility\n\n- Enabled by default through `share_out_dir`. Use `MBX_SHARE_OUT_DIR=0 mbx build` or `share_out_dir = false` in `.mbx.toml` to preserve Cargo's original `OUT_DIR` and generated source paths.\n- Sharing requires a detectable `OUT_DIR` reference and output mbx can copy. Missed references, unsupported output such as symlinks, and copy failures retain Cargo's original directory. Reuse across machines also requires matching absolute cache paths.\n- Cached files are marked read-only. Code that modifies generated output during compilation should disable sharing. Build scripts still produce their output in Cargo's original directory.\n- `mbx cache stats` reports copies as **generated source trees**. Automatic collection and `mbx gc` age them using `target.max_age`; a later compilation recreates an evicted copy. Embedded `OUT_DIR` paths are not permanent runtime storage.\n- The setting also controls generated source path remapping in Rust and C/C++ debug information. The shared directory is supplied to Rust compilations; C/C++ continues to use the original build-script output directory.\n\n### Validation\n\nIntegration coverage checks reuse across two checkouts, different generated output, dependent-crate reuse, and Cargo freshness after restoration. Unit coverage checks tree identity, executable bits, symlink fallback, read-only file permissions, source scanning, and age-based collection.\n\nThe documentation build, internal page/fragment checks, regenerated CLI reference, and Rust formatting check pass. The local full `mise run ci` attempt stops at `test:pretty` because the Python shim invokes mise 2026.9.1, below the repository's required 2026.9.2; it did not complete the full suite.\n\n<!-- entire-trail-link-start -->\nhttps://entire.io/gh/jdx/mr-boxington/trails/10\n<!-- entire-trail-link-end -->\n","headRefOid":"81b9102a30435e869012e2404fef8f4bbebddc19","title":"feat(cache): reuse OUT_DIR compilations across checkouts","url":"https://github.com/jdx/mr-boxington/pull/498"}
"}
